          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.172,
            0.813,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "19. What type of chemical bond is present in a solid compound that does not conduct electricity in the solid state but conducts electricity when dissolved in water?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.206,
            0.812,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "20. Metal M, with two electrons in its valence shell (M shell), reacts with oxygen to form a compound that is slightly soluble in water. Predict its:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.239,
            0.27,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) formula"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.257,
            0.305,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) type of bond"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.274,
            0.57,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) electrical conductivity of its aqueous solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.323,
            0.671,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.7 Molecular Mass of Covalent Compounds"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.348,
            0.777,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since you know the formulae of the covalent compounds, you can find the masses of their molecules by simply adding up the masses of the atoms present in them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.785,
            0.311,
            0.952,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.785,
                0.311,
                0.952,
                0.428
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.405,
            0.242,
            0.423
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 9.4",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.426,
            0.387,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Molecular mass of water "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( \\mathrm { H } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { O } )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.448,
            0.402,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atomic mass — H = 1 u; O = 16 u"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.82,
            0.431,
            0.914,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) Camphor"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.467,
            0.566,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Molecular mass of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{H_2O} = (1\\mathrm{~u} \\times 2) + (16\\mathrm{~u} \\times 1) = 18\\mathrm{~u}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.492,
            0.242,
            0.51
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 9.5"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.513,
            0.465,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Molecular mass of carbon dioxide (CO )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.534,
            0.409,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atomic mass — C = 12 u; O = 16 u"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.554,
            0.575,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Molecular Mass of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{CO}_{2}=(12\\mathrm{~u}\\times1)+(16\\mathrm{~u}\\times2)=44\\mathrm{~u}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.575,
            0.773,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have learnt in section 9.4.2 that in ionic compounds, the ions form 3-D crystals, i.e., ionic compounds do not form molecules."
            }
          ]
        },
